\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Parity and reversal separate on bivectors}
\lean/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.reverse_bivector,CliffordAlgebra.reverse_bivectorExterior}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative ring with invertible #{2}, let #{M} be an
#{R}-module, and let #{Q} be a quadratic form on #{M}. Write
#{\alpha} for the grading involution and #{\operatorname{rev}} for
reversal. For the half-commutator bivector of \ref{fcap-000N},

##{\begin{aligned}
\alpha(\beta_Q(u,v))&=\beta_Q(u,v),\\
\operatorname{rev}(\beta_Q(u,v))&=-\beta_Q(u,v).
\end{aligned}}

The same reversal sign holds for every bivector in the image of
#{\bigwedge_R^2 M}; reversal acts as minus the identity there.}

\p{Chevalley defines the main antiautomorphism by reversing products
while fixing generators \citet{Section 2.1, p. 38}{chevalley1954algebraic}.
His parity decomposition identifies even exterior degree with the
even Clifford part \citet{II.1.6, p. 42}{chevalley1954algebraic}.
Those are finite-dimensional field-space conventions; the displayed
bivector statement is Tau Ceti's commutative-ring extension.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{Parity changes the sign of each generator, so it fixes a product of
two. Reversal exchanges the order of those generators. Applied to the
half-commutator, the exchange negates it. Linearity then gives the same
sign on the exterior-square image
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.reverse_bivectorExterior}.
The bivector is even
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.bivector_mem_evenOdd_zero};
on any even element reversal also agrees with Clifford conjugation
#{x\mapsto x^*}, since parity is the identity there
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.reverse_eq_star_of_mem_even}.}
}
